      "id": 1,
      "text": "St. Charles was originally called Woodruff, named after a local landowner, before adopting its current name in 1874 when it was incorporated.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/st_charles_kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "St. Charles began as a coal mining camp in 1872 on land bought from J.I.D., anchoring the town’s early economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Charles%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Louisville & Nashville Railroad ran through Hopkins County, linking St. Charles to regional markets and fueling its mining boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/st_charles_kentucky",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the Civil War, Union soldiers settled in St. Charles, helping to rebuild the community while mining expanded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/st_charles_kentucky",
